  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nMoisture and atmospheric forcing will peak Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday night as the main upper trough axis lifts over \nthe Rockies and gradually over the Central/Southern Plains. This \nwill help push a strong surface cold front to near or just south \nof the OK/KS border Wednesday night before stalling. The front is \nexpected to briefly lift northward Thursday morning as a surface \nlow lifts towards the Midwest Region. On the backside of the \ndeparting surface low, the frontal boundary is forecast to push \nthrough the CWA starting late Thursday afternoon (or early \nevening) and continue to slide through the CWA late Thursday \nnight. Both ECMWF ENS and NAEFS ENS show extreme moisture flux, \nproducing anomalously high IVT values exceeding 500-750 kg/m/s and\nPWATs near or at record values across eastern OK and western AR \non Wednesday into Thursday. This intense moisture transport, \ncoupled with strong synoptic lift and frontal convergence will \nsupport pronounced, heavy convective rainfall along and ahead of \nthe boundary. As mentioned in the previous discussion, dewpoint \ntemperatures are forecast to spike into the low to middle 70s \nWednesday afternoon. With plenty of instability and sufficient \nbulk wind shear in place, a few strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms may form ahead of the approaching front during the \ndaytime Wednesday, mostly focused near and north of I-40. \nMarginally severe hail and damaging wind gusts will be the primary\nhazards with severe storms.\n\nThe National Blend of Models and global ensembles continue to \nhighlight high rainfall probabilities (70-90% PoPs), but have \ntrended total rainfall amounts lower than the previous forecast \npackage. Consensus in model data still show the highest \nprobabilities of precipitation and highest rainfall amounts near \nand north of I-44 basically from Wednesday morning through Thursday \nafternoon. Overall, updated storm-total rainfall amounts of 1 to 3\ninches, with localized higher totals up to 5 inches, are \nanticipated, mainly for areas along and north of I-44 through \nFriday morning. At this time, locations south of I-44 are expected\nto receive significantly less rainfall, generally less than an\ninch. Given the overall decrease in rainfall totals, will opt not\nto issue a Flood Watch at this time, though heavy rainfall may \nstill cause minor flooding at times. An impromptu Flood Watch may \nstill need to be considered for portions of northeast OK if QPF \nvalues trend higher over the next 24 hours.\n\nThe cold front will flush the moisture plume eastward late\nThursday night and into Friday. Post-frontal high pressure\nbuilding south from Canada will yield a fairly pleasant autumn\nairmass Friday through at least through the upcoming weekend, \nwith well below normal temperatures on Friday and Saturday. \nTemperatures will then trend warmer and possibly back above\naverage by Monday. Expect mostly dry conditions, mostly clear to \npartly cloudy skies, and daytime highs trending towards the upper\n70s and lower 80s by Monday. Overnight lows are forecast to drop \ninto the upper 30s and 40s over the upcoming weekend.\n\nMejia",
